# Line endings are LF in the repository *and* in the working tree, on every
# platform.
#
# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` — the usual Windows default, and what a
# fresh Git for Windows install sets — checks every text file out with CRLF
# while storing LF. The repository stays clean, but the working copy no longer
# matches what the tools expect: `gofmt -l .` reports all 56 Go files as
# unformatted, so the one command that tells you whether your code is formatted
# tells you nothing, and `make fmt` rewrites every file in the tree. CLAUDE.md
# documented that as a Windows quirk to work around; it is a setting, and this
# is the setting.
#
# text=auto still lets Git detect binaries rather than corrupting them.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Belt and braces for the two cases where a stray CR is not cosmetic: a CRLF
# shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter whose name ends in \r, and a
# CRLF Dockerfile RUN line carries the CR into the container's shell.
*.sh  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Never normalize. text=auto would classify this correctly on its own; saying
# so explicitly means a future rule cannot accidentally capture it.
*.png binary
